{"challenges":[{"attack":"Test the factorization at arbitrary real x by expansion, including x=2.","claim_id":"factorization","outcome":"survives","result":"(x-2)(x+2)=x²+2x-2x-4=x²-4 for every real x.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Check whether any real x other than 2 makes the denominator zero, and whether x=2 is excluded.","claim_id":"original-domain","outcome":"survives","result":"x-2=0 if and only if x=2. Thus the quotient is defined exactly on ℝ\\{2}.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Check cancellation at the excluded point and on the stated domain.","claim_id":"simplification","outcome":"survives","result":"For x≠2, x-2 is nonzero, so cancellation gives x+2. At x=2 cancellation is invalid because the original denominator is zero; the claim is correctly restricted to the original domain.","tool_ids":[]},{"attack":"Compare both expressions at the possible exceptional point x=2.","claim_id":"not-global-equivalence","outcome":"survives","result":"At x=2, x+2=4 is defined, while (x²-4)/(x-2) is undefined. Hence they are not equivalent as expressions at every real number.","tool_ids":[]}],"checks":[{"basis_reasoning":"The factor-step supplies a direct expansion establishing the asserted polynomial identity, with no unproved external result required.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["factor-step"],"claim_id":"factorization","reasoning":"Expanding the right-hand side yields x²-4, so the identity holds for all real x.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"The domain-step directly analyzes the sole denominator and establishes precisely when it is nonzero.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["domain-step"],"claim_id":"original-domain","reasoning":"The numerator is a polynomial defined for every real x, and the denominator is nonzero exactly when x≠2.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"The factor-step and domain-step establish both the factorization and the required nonzero condition for cancellation; cancel-step applies them with the correct restriction.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["factor-step","domain-step","cancel-step"],"claim_id":"simplification","reasoning":"For every x in ℝ\\{2}, substitution of the factorization gives ((x-2)(x+2))/(x-2), and division by the nonzero factor x-2 gives x+2.","verdict":"supported"},{"basis_reasoning":"The domain-step establishes that the original is undefined at 2, and non-equivalence-step directly evaluates the simplified polynomial there. The extra dependency on simplification is unnecessary but does not make the argument circular.","basis_verdict":"applicable","checked_step_ids":["domain-step","non-equivalence-step"],"claim_id":"not-global-equivalence","reasoning":"The expressions have different value-status at x=2: the original is undefined and x+2 has value 4. Therefore they cannot be equivalent at every real number.","verdict":"supported"}],"missing_evidence":[],"recommended_action":"finish","tool_requests":[]}
